schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: MakerDAO's early MKR accumulation gave outsized governance influence
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    In MakerDAO, users stake ETH as collateral so their financial interest
    aligns with system stability, but early investors accumulated significant
    MKR (the governance token), giving them outsized influence over voting on
    interest rates and accepted collateral assets — and the article states
    this pattern ("this happens more often than people admit") recurs across
    DAOs generally.
  evidence: >-
    Article's own description of MakerDAO's mechanics and governance history;
    no external citation or dataset is linked for the "outsized influence"
    claim specifically.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/tokens-coordination-tools.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used as the primary real-world example of the "wealth concentration"
    challenge in token-based coordination — illustrating that
    token-alignment-with-system-stability (staking) does not by itself solve
    governance-power concentration.
  uncertainty: >-
    No quantitative source (e.g., on-chain MKR distribution data) is cited to
    support "significant"/"outsized" — this is an asserted pattern, not a
    measured one, in the source article.
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/tokens-coordination-tools.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
